\version "2.26.0"
% c494 (Adler part 2 PDF 184, Bloch: a crescendo roll is marked piano and then crescendo, a loud roll would obliterate the rest; PDF 162, 164: a soft chord must be under control): 209 the timpani A flat roll takes the upper woodwinds' fp < (c216) instead of f <, as the roll took the brass sfp < at 149 (c346); the f attack stays, the two-bar swell into 211 is heard from p.
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 102): 207-210 Tbn 2 holds E flat3 (was G flat3, a seventh over the bass trombone's A flat2 for four bars); the seventh G flat stays in Hn 2 (G flat4, the player who carries it on as F sharp4 at 219) and in Bn 1, violas.
% CODA, bars 199-210. 199-204: the last C-minor tutti, in movement I's hammered layout exactly as at its bars 27-32 (movement I's first-theme
% head in four octaves, double basses in repeated eighths on the chromatic bass C-D-E flat-E-F, bassoon 1 and cellos on the tenor line,
% horns and trombones held, trumpets and timpani in the c4 r8 c8 rhythm; cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 7). Movement I's head is used only as a link.
% 205-210: over the A flat pedal (V of D flat) theme F1's head in the horns, then in the high winds and violins; A flat 7 crescendo into D flat.

% c042 (harmony; A&S PDF 376, Ex. 22-5, transferred resolution): 210 Fl 1 and Cl 1 release the 7th G flat on beat 3, where Vln I/II reach F (was G flat held against F in the same octaves).
% c088 (expression; Adler ch. 10, PDF 309: a group phrases and breathes as one; PDF 305 Ex. X-12/X-13: the whole section shares the hairpin): 209-210 Vln I, Vln II and Cb join the crescendo into 211 that every other part has (they went from f to the ff of 211 without one); 204 Tpt 3 ff with Tpt 1-2 on the same accented quarters.
% c105 (Gould PDF 35: an interval of a second beyond the ledger lines takes a 1/4-space slope, and beams outside the stave match the angles of the others): 199 Fl 1 D6-D flat 6-C6 (stems up on the shared staff) was horizontal; now 1/4 space down like Vln I (in the part, stems down, the Vln I values).
% c178 (expression; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 185: a weak register cannot be played louder, so it must not be covered; PDF 210, Ex. VII-56: the clarinet's throat tones are "rather pale"; PDF 226: an accompanied bassoon is swallowed up): 207-208 the horns and Tbn 1-2 hold mp (were mf). The head's lower octave (Cl 1-2 A flat4-C4 from the throat B flat, Bn 1 A flat3) lies inside the held chord (Hn 4 E flat4, Hn 2 G flat4, Hn 3 C5, Tbn 1 C4); the crescendo from 209 is unchanged, so the build into 211 now starts lower. Pitches unchanged.
% c177 (texture; Adler ch. 8, PDF 251 Rossini: the eight-foot/four-foot principle; PDF 256: the prominent melody note in a good register for its instrument; PDF 257: held parts must not upset the flow of the melody; PDF 267 Brahms 1/ii: a single bassoon doubling the theme in octaves brings it through a thick harmonic background):
%   207-208 since c176 the F1 head's upper octave had Fl 1, Ob 1 and Vln I at A flat 5 and the lower octave only Cl 1, starting on the throat B flat (written) under Cl 2's held A flat 4, which it then crossed on the shared staff; Fl 2's held E flat 5 likewise lay over Fl 1's D flat 5-C5.
%   Now Cl 1-2 play the head a2 at A flat 4 (A flat 4 stays held in the violas), Bn 1 doubles it an octave lower at A flat 3 (as Tbn 1 did under the horns at 205-206; its E flat 4 stays in Hn 4 and the violas), and Fl 2 rests 207-208, entering f < at 209 with Fl 1. Pitches of F1 unchanged.
% c216 (playability, woodwind envelope; Adler ch. 6, adler1 PDF 178 Ex. VI-10/11: the attack-drop-swell envelope fp < f is best done by the woodwinds; PDF 177 Ex. VI-9, Mendelssohn 4/i: winds hold fp, then f):
%   209-210 Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2, Cl 1-2 take the A flat 7 at fp < (was f <, which left them only f to ff); the violins' run A flat 4-G flat 6 rises through the dip and the swell into 211 is heard over two bars. fp, not sf: the strong marks stay limited (c008).
% c295 (Gould PDF 51: a rest stands at the level of the surrounding pitches; PDF 50: it moves out only as far as the other part requires): 210.3 Fl 1's
% crotchet rest stood eight spaces above the centre, two above its own G flat 6, with nothing of Fl 2 at that beat; moved down two spaces, level with the G flat 6 (score only).
% c302 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 264-266 and 279: a sequence repeats a pattern on another degree, rising by step, with chord qualities adjusted to the key;
%   PDF 265: sequential Fortspinnung as the connective passage into a goal): 207-208 F1's head is restated a step higher, on B flat minor (B flat-F-G flat-A flat | G flat-F-E flat-D flat),
%   instead of literally at 205-206's pitch, as the exposition's 35-38 (A flat, then B flat minor). Over the A flat pedal the held chord becomes ii7 (E flat minor 7): Hn 3 B flat 4, Tbn 1 D flat 4
%   (the seventh, resolving to C4 at 209), Ob 2 B flat 4; Vln II's run ends on G flat 5. 205-206 V7 - 207-208 ii7 - 209-210 V7 - 211 I. Scoring, dynamics and rhythm unchanged.
% c392 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 267 sec. 5: "normally ... all the voices above a sequential bass are themselves sequential"; PDF 265-266: the second statement repeats the first on the next degree, qualities adjusted to the key):
%   207-208 Vln II's scale run moves up a step with the head and the held chord (B flat3-C4-D flat4-E flat4-F4-G flat4-A flat4-B flat4 | C5-D flat5-E flat5-F5, G flat5 accented), so the run, like every other voice, is 205-206's a step higher (205-206 run A flat-F, 207-208 B flat-G flat). It was left at 205's degree (A flat3 ... E flat5, then a skip to G flat5), so the one moving inner line did not take part in c302's sequence and repeated 209's run note for note. 208.2.5 F5 now doubles the head's passing F instead of an E flat5 a second under it; the accented G flat5 on 208.3, the rhythm, slur, dynamic and 209-210 are unchanged.
codaOneFluteOne = { \partCombineApart r8\ff g''8( es'''4.->) \tag #'score { \once \override Beam.positions = #'(8 . 7.75) } \tag #'part { \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) } d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8( c'''8) aes''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) es'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | bes''8-. c'''-. des'''-. bes''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | aes'''4.-> g'''8 f'''4 d'''4 | c'''4-> b''4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | bes''2->(\f f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) des''4-. | ges'''1\fp\< | ges'''2 \tag #'score { \once \override Rest.extra-offset = #'(0 . -2) } r4 r4\! | }
% c105 (shared staves need equal simultaneous dynamics): 210 Fl 1 and Cl 1 end their crescendo on beat 4 with Fl 2 and Cl 2 (it ended on beat 3, Fl 2/Cl 2 on beat 4: two hairpin ends on one staff). Sound unchanged.
codaOneFluteTwo = { \partCombineApart \tag #'score { g''2\ff~ g''2 | aes''2~ aes''2 | aes''2~ aes''2 | bes''2~ bes''2 } \tag #'part { g''1\ff | aes''1 | aes''1 | bes''1 } | c'''2 f''2 | g''4-> g''4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | R1*2 | es'''1\fp\< | es'''2. r4\! | }
% c176 (playability; Sebesky ch. 2, PDF 71: the oboe's lowest notes F4-B flat3 are coarse and hard to control; PDF 79-80: oboe low register harsh under the flute, flute/oboe unison from C5 up strong): 207-208 Ob 1 plays F1's head an octave higher (A flat5-C5, unison with Fl 1 and Vln I; was A flat4-C4 under Ob 2's C5), which also removes the C4-C6 leap into 209.
% c176 (playability; Sebesky ch. 2, PDF 70: allow breathing spaces; PDF 39: tuba): 205-210 Tuba and Cbsn breathe after 208 (six bars of low A flat f-crescendo at 2 = 88-92); Bass Tbn and Cb hold the pedal through.
codaOneOboeOne = { \partCombineApart r8\ff g'8( es''4.->) d''8( des''8 c''8 | c''4--) b'8( c''8) aes'4.-> g'8 | r8 g'8( f''4.->) es''8( d''8 c''8) | bes'8-. c''-. des''-. bes'-. g''4.-> f''8 | aes''4.-> g''8 f''4 d''4 | c''4-> b'4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | bes''2->(\f f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) des''4-. | c'''1\fp\< | c'''2. r4\! | }
codaOneOboeTwo = { \partCombineApart \tag #'score { g'2\ff~ g'2 | f'2~ f'2 | aes'2~ aes'2 | g'2~ g'2 } \tag #'part { g'1\ff | f'1 | aes'1 | g'1 } | aes'2 f'2 | f'4-> f'4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | bes'1\f | bes'2 r2 | aes''1\fp\< | aes''2. r4\! | }
codaOneClOne = { \partCombineApart r8\ff g'8( es''4.->) d''8( des''8 c''8 | c''4--) b'8( c''8) aes'4.-> g'8 | r8 g'8( f''4.->) es''8( d''8 c''8) | bes'8-. c''-. des''-. bes'-. g''4.-> f''8 | aes''4.-> g''8 f''4 d''4 | c''4-> b'4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | bes'2->(\f f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) des'4-. | ges''1\fp\< | ges''2 r4 r4\! | }
codaOneClTwo = { \partCombineApart es'1\ff | d'1 | c'1 | des'1 | c'2 d'2 | d'4-> d'4-> r2 | \partCombineAutomatic R1*2 | bes'2->(\f f'4. ges'8 | aes'4-.) ges'8( f'8 es'4-.) des'4-. | es''1\fp\< | es''2. r4\! | }
codaOneBsnOne = { c'2\ff es'2 | d'2 f'2 | es'2 c'2 | e'2 bes2 | aes2 f2 | g4-> g,4-> r2 | c'1\f | c'1 | bes2->( f4. ges8 | aes4-.) ges8( f8 es4-.) des4-. | ges1\fp\< | ges2. r4\! | }
% c387 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 74: horns above trombones in octaves (4 Hn / 2 Tbn) is 'not so reliable'; PDF 74-75: a wood-wind in unison with a brass instrument softens it and 'Trombone + Fag.' gives a finer legato): 205-206 Bn 2 plays F1's head with Tbn 1-2 at A flat3 (it doubled the A flat2 of B. tbn and Vc), so the trombone octave under the four horns is sweetened toward the horn tone; Bn 1 takes the head at 207-208 with the wood-wind, so the bassoons pass it on. Bn 2 returns to the A flat2 pedal at 207.
codaOneBsnTwo = { c1\ff | d1 | es1 | e1 | f2 d2 | g,4-> g,4-> r2 | aes2->(\f es4. f8 | ges4-.) f8( es8 des4-.) c4-. | aes,1 | aes,1 | aes,1\fp\< | aes,2. r4\! | }
% c486 (playability; Adler ch. 7, adler1 PDF 230: the contrabassoon's low notes take much breath, give it periodic rests; it is a 32-foot foundation when it doubles an octave
%   below, not at the unison): 205-208 Cbsn rests (it doubled the tuba's and basses' A flat1 at the unison) and re-enters f < on A flat1 at 209 with the crescendo into 211, so its 199-230 run
%   (32 bars, breaths only) is broken by four bars' rest. A flat1 stays in tuba and basses, A flat2 in bass trombone and Bn 2.
codaOneCbsn = { c,4\ff c,4 c,4 c,4 | d,4 d,4 d,4 d,4 | es,4 es,4 es,4 es,4 | e,4 e,4 e,4 e,4 | f,4 f,4 d,4 d,4 | g,,4-> g,,4-> r2 | R1*4 | aes,,1\f\< | aes,,2. r4\! | }
% c104 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108-109: forte horns overpower wood-wind; PDF 110: wood-wind above the brass; PDF 112: brass one degree softer): 207-208 horns and Tbn 1-2 mf under the wood-wind/violin F1 head (f), crescendo from 209 as before.
% c136 (playability; Adler ch. 10, PDF 304 leaps, PDF 303: the high register is taxing): 206 beat 4 Hn 1 and Hn 3 rest (Hn 2 and Hn 4 keep the head's last C4), so the high horns are not taken a tenth (Hn 1, to written B flat 5) or an octave (Hn 3) straight into the held mf chord at 207.
% c316 (playability; Adler ch. 9-10, adler1 PDF 284: soft attacks in the extreme high register are 'fiendishly difficult'; PDF 303, Ex. X-5: written E5-C6 is the 'brilliant and loud' register,
%   the high register is taxing; PDF 301 Ex. X-2 and PDF 303: I and III high, II and IV low, the third between the first and second): 207-210 Hn 1 no longer holds written B flat5 (E flat5) mp for four bars
%   after a quarter rest; the chord is A flat3 (Hn 4) - E flat4 (Hn 2) - G flat4 (Hn 3) - B flat4/C5 (Hn 1), interlocked 1-3-2-4. E flat stays in Hn 2, Tbn 2, the violas and (209-210) Cl 2 / Fl 2.
% c346 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 41: horns are marked one degree stronger than trumpets and trombones to balance them): Hn 1-4 mf at 207 in the chord with Tbn 1-2 mp
% c586 (playability/attack; Adler ch. 9, adler1 PDF 290 Ex. IX-9: the forte-piano attack and crescendo, sff>pp<ff, in four horns, trumpets, trombones and tuba; adler2 PDF 8 Ex. X-87; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129, the dip must be audible, as at 149): 209 the held brass (Hn 1-4, Tbn 1-2, bass trombone, tuba) take the woodwinds' and timpani's fp < on the A flat 7 (were mf/mp/f <), so six held brass in the same chord no longer fill the dip; Bn 1-2 and the violas' held chord take it too, so the swell into 211 starts from p in every sustained part (the A flat pedal keeps f in Cb and Cbsn, the runs keep f <). Notes, scoring and the 211 ff unchanged.
codaOneHnOne = { g'1\ff | f'1 | aes'1 | g'1 | aes'2 aes'2 | g'4-> g'4-> r2 | aes'2->(\f es'4. f'8 | ges'4-.) f'8( es'8 des'4-.) r4 | bes'1\mf | bes'1 | c''1\fp\< | c''2. r4\! | }
% c046 (Adler PDF 301, Ex. X-2: the low horn's top is written G5; PDF 303: I and III high, II and IV low): 207-210 Hn 3 takes C5 and Hn 2 G flat 4 (were swapped); same pitches
codaOneHnTwo = { es'1\ff | f'1 | f'1 | g'1 | f'2 f'2 | f'4-> f'4-> r2 | aes'2->(\f es'4. f'8 | ges'4-.) f'8( es'8 des'4-.) c'4-. | es'1\mf | es'1 | es'1\fp\< | es'2. r4\! | }
codaOneHnThree = { c'1\ff\>~ | c'4-\tag #'score -\tweak extra-offset #'(-0.3 . -1.6) \f b2. | c'1 | des'1 | c'2 c'2 | c'4-> b4-> r2 | aes'2->(\f es'4. f'8 | ges'4-.) f'8( es'8 des'4-.) r4 | ges'1\mf | ges'1 | ges'1\fp\< | ges'2. r4\! | }
codaOneHnFour = { g1\ff\> | g2-\tag #'score -\tweak extra-offset #'(-0.3 . -1.6) \f r2 | aes1 | bes1 | aes2 aes2 | g4-> g4-> r2 | aes'2->(\f es'4. f'8 | ges'4-.) f'8( es'8 des'4-.) c'4-. | aes1\mf | aes1 | aes1\fp\< | aes2. r4\! | }
% c108 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: after a long rest horns, trombones and tuba re-enter pp or ff, a forte re-entry is less successful; PDF 129: the sf-dim attack): 199 the brass that re-enter after 32 bars (Hn 3-4, Tpt 1-3, Tbn 1-2, B.tbn, tuba) attack ff; the held parts diminish to f by 200 (the c104 balance from 200 on is kept), the trumpets stay ff with the timpani in the same rhythm (the duplicate ff at 204 removed). Pitches unchanged (movement I 27-32).
% follows movement I v225 (cycle 224, Rimsky PDF 82-84): the inner brass at I 28-31 no longer double the chromatic bass D-E flat-E-F; same notes here at 200-203 (Tpt 2, Tpt 3, Hn 2).
codaOneTptOne = { c''4\ff^\markup \italic "senza sord." r8 c''8 c''4 r8 c''8 | g'4 r4 b'4 r4 | c''1 | des''4 r8 des''8 des''4 r8 des''8 | c''2 aes'2 | g'4-> g'4-> r2 | R1*6 | }
% 200 beat 3 Tpt 2 F4 (vii°4/3 with Tpt 1 B4), following movement I v303 bar 28 at pitch
codaOneTptTwo = { g'4\ff r8 g'8 g'4 r8 g'8 | g'4 r4 f'4 r4 | aes'1 | bes'4 r8 bes'8 bes'4 r8 bes'8 | aes'2 f'2 | f'4-> f'4-> r2 | R1*6 | }
codaOneTptThree = { es'4\ff r8 es'8 es'4 r8 es'8 | f'4 r4 f'4 r4 | f'1 | g'4 r8 g'8 g'4 r8 g'8 | aes'2 f'2 | d'4-> d'4-> r2 | R1*6 | }
codaOneTbnOne = { c'1\ff\>~ | c'4\f b2. | c'1 | des'1 | c'2 c'2 | c'4-> b4-> r2 | aes2->(\f es4. f8 | ges4-.) f8( es8 des4-.) c4-. | des'1\mp | des'1 | c'1\fp\< | c'2. r4\! | }
% c381 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 74: horns above trombones in octaves go 2 Horns over 1 Trombone or 4 Horns over 2 Trombones): 205-206 Tbn 2 joins Tbn 1 on F1's head (A flat3) under Hn 1-4 a2 (was a held E flat3; E flat stays in the violas and the head). 207-210 unchanged.
codaOneTbnTwo = { g1\ff\> | f1\f | aes1 | g1 | aes2 aes2 | g4-> g4-> r2 | aes2->(\f es4. f8 | ges4-.) f8( es8 des4-.) c4-. | es1\mp | es1 | es1\fp\< | es2. r4\! | }
codaOneBassTbn = { c1\ff\> | d1\f | es1 | e1 | f2 d2 | g,4-> g,4-> r2 | aes,1\f | aes,1 | aes,1 | aes,1 | aes,1\fp\< | aes,2. r4\! | }
codaOneTuba = { c,1\ff\> | d,1\f | es,1 | e,1 | f,2 d,2 | g,,4-> g,,4-> r2 | aes,,1\f | aes,,1 | aes,,1 | aes,,1 \breathe | aes,,1\fp\< | aes,,2. r4\! | }
% c236 (playability; Adler ch. 12, PDF 79: the timpani strengthen the tonic and dominant bass notes and consummate climactic cadences; PDF 79-81 and Ex. XII-51: pedal drums retune quickly,
% mark the change in the part): the C drum (26", last struck at 202) goes to D-flat 3 here, so the A-flat roll 209-210 resolves to the D-flat tonic with the bass at 211 (V-I in D-flat).
% Same wording as the scherzo's "muta G in G#". D-flat is also a drum pitch of movement II, whose theme B 211-218 recalls. Back to C at 215.
codaOneTimp = { c4\ff_\markup \small \italic "ord." r8 c8 c4 r8 c8 | g,4 r4 g,4 r4 | R1 | c4 r8 c8 c4 r8 c8 | R1 | g,4-> g,4-> r2 | aes,4->\f r4 r2 | R1^\markup \small \italic "muta C in D♭" | aes,4-> r4 r2 | R1 | aes,1:32\fp\< | aes,2.:32 r4\! | }
codaOnePerc = \drummode { R1*12 | }
% c010: no cymbal on 210 b4; the movement's first crash is the arrival at 219 (the D flat episode is a stop on the way, A&S PDF 589, 593).
% c105 (engraving; Gould PDF 35: when every note of a beamed group lies beyond the first ledger line the beam takes a slight slope, 1/2 space for intervals wider than a second;
% PDF 36: the beam follows the outer interval): 206 and 210 Vln I B flat 5-C6-D flat 6-E flat 6 rose under a horizontal beam (LilyPond kept both stems on the middle line); now 1/2 space up, as at 222 and 246.
% c148: 199 the strings' crescendo from 198 ends on the downbeat rest (\!), so no hairpin stub prints before the ff on the second eighth.
% c151 (melody; A&S Unit 21, PDF 357-358 sec. 18 and Ex. 21-23a: the 7th of V7 suspended as a 4-3 over I; PDF 352 sec. 12: the tone of resolution is not sounded before the suspension resolves): 210 the violins' run ends on the 7th G flat (G flat 6 / G flat 5; was F6/F5, which sounded the 7th's resolution a bar early while the winds' G flat 6 was still held), and Vln I ties G flat 6 over the caesura into 211, where it resolves to F6 on beat 2 (codaTwo.ily); Vln II goes on to A flat 5 as before.
% c156: 210 Vln I G flat 6 is one half note tied into 211 (was two quarters tied inside 210 and restruck at 211, so the c151 suspension was not tied)
% c157 (texture; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 12-13, Ex. 1: an inner line in the soloist's register was cut, so the remaining voices and the solo came through; PDF 13: focus, no over-busy background on the primary element):
%   205-206 Vln II plays the run in unison with Vln I (was an octave lower, A flat 3-A flat 4 through the horns' F1 head, at the unison or a second from it, and on Tbn 1's A flat 3); from 207 Vln II takes the lower octave again under the woodwind/Vln I head, as c107 set it.
%   205-206 violas div. A flat 4/C5 (was E flat 4/A flat 4, the head's own first two pitches struck six times a bar inside its register); 207-210 unchanged.
% c218 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38 and Ex. II-19/II-20: an anacrusis is taken up-bow unless a down-bow is marked, and two up-bows are hooked so the accent falls on a down-bow; PDF 42-43, Ex. II-29: a series of down-bows, the bow lifted, for heavy separate strokes): 199-204 take movement I's own bowing of its hammered tutti (I 27-32, mvt1 v221 tOne.ily): Vln I, Vln II, Vla down-bow on 199's pickup, hooked up-bow on 200's B eighth, so the accents E flat (199) and A flat (200) fall on down-bows; all five string parts take 204's two accented quarters as two down-bows, as at I 32. 247-254 (c186) already bow the major version this way. Pitches, slurs and dynamics unchanged.
% c418 (Adler PDF 38): 209 down-bow (retaken after the staccato D-flat at 208.4) so the accented G-flat6 at 210.3 and its tie into the ff at 211 are down-bow, as in Vln II and the cellos.
codaOneVlnOne = { r8\! g''8\downbow(\ff es'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8\upbow( c'''8) aes''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) es'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | bes''8-. c'''-. des'''-. bes''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | aes'''4.-> g'''8 f'''4 d'''4 | c'''4->\downbow b''4->\downbow r2 | aes'8(\f bes' c'' des'') es''( f'' ges'' aes'' | \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.25 . 0.25) bes''8 c''' des''' es''') f'''4-> r4 | bes''2->( f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) des''4-. | aes'8(\<\downbow bes' c'' des'') es''( f'' ges'' aes'' | \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.25 . 0.25) bes''8 c''' des''' es''') ges'''2->~ | }
% c107 (texture; Adler ch. 5, PDF 144 points 3-4 and PDF 147 Weber: theme and counter-figure stated together, apart in rhythm): 207-208 Vln II keeps the rising run under F1's head (Fl, Ob, Cl 1, Vln I), so the head and the run sound together instead of in turn (205-210 run continuous in Vln II).
% c158 (expression; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 13-14: one primary element, the background must not divert the ear from it; ch. 6, PDF 211: a background in the tune's register distorts the focus): 207-208 Vln II (run A flat 3-F5, through Ob 1/Cl 1's head at A flat 4-C4) and the violas' repeated chord mf, as c104 set the horns and Tbn 1-2 under the woodwind/Vln I head (f); both crescendo from 209 as before. Pitches unchanged.
codaOneVlnTwo = { r8\! g'8\downbow(\ff es''4.->) d''8( des''8 c''8 | c''4--) b'8\upbow( c''8) aes'4.-> g'8 | r8 g'8( f''4.->) es''8( d''8 c''8) | bes'8-. c''-. des''-. bes'-. g''4.-> f''8 | aes''4.-> g''8 f''4 d''4 | c''4->\downbow b'4->\downbow r2 | aes'8(\f bes' c'' des'') es''( f'' ges'' aes'' | \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0.25) Beam bes''8 c''' des''' es''') f'''4-> r4 | bes8(\mf c' des' es') f'( ges' aes' bes' | c''8 des'' es'' f'') ges''4-> r4 | aes8(\< bes c' des') es'( f' ges' aes' | bes'8 c'' des'' es'') ges''4-> r4\! | }
% c159 (Adler PDF 144 no. 3): 205-206 violas div. C5/E flat 5, above the horns' F1 head (A flat 4 down to C4), not on its first note.
% c535 (engraving; Adler ch. 2, PDF 30, Ex. II-12: unmarked double notes are double-stops, "it is safer to write non div."): 204 violas "non div." on the two ff down-bow strokes D4/F4, so they do not read as the "div." that starts at 205. Text only; MIDI unchanged.
codaOneVla = { r8\! g8\downbow(\ff es'4.->) d'8( des'8 c'8 | c'4--) b8_\upbow( c'8) aes4.-> g8 | r8 g8( f'4.->) es'8( d'8 c'8) | bes8-. c'-. des'-. bes-. g'4.-> f'8 | aes'4.-> g'8 f'4 d'4 | <d' f'>4->\downbow^\markup \italic "non div." <d' f'>4->\downbow r2 | <c'' es''>4\f^\markup \italic "div." r8 <c'' es''>8 <c'' es''>4 r8 <c'' es''>8 | <c'' es''>4 r8 <c'' es''>8 <c'' es''>4 r8 <c'' es''>8 | <es' aes'>4\mf r8 <es' aes'>8 <es' aes'>4 r8 <es' aes'>8 | <es' aes'>4 r8 <es' aes'>8 <es' aes'>4 r8 <es' aes'>8 | <es' ges'>1\fp\< | <es' ges'>2. r4\! | }
% c407 (texture; Adler ch. 5, adler1 PDF 137 Ex. V-14, Beethoven Leonore 3: an orchestral crescendo built by adding octaves of the running figure; PDF 141 Ex. V-19: cellos double the violins' line in octaves): 209-210 the cellos leave the A flat pedal (kept in Cb, Bn 2, Cbsn, Bass Tbn, tuba, timpani) and double Vln II's run an octave lower, A flat 2-G flat 4, so the third run of 205-210 sounds in three octaves (Vln I, Vln II, Vc) into the ff at 211. Harmony, top line and dynamics unchanged.
% c418 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 38-39, Ex. II-21/II-22: at forte only about six notes on a bow; strong attacks on a down-bow): 209-210 the cellos take Vln II's bowing an octave below (4 + 8 eighths), as c407 intended, instead of 12 eighths on one bow; the accented G-flat4 at 210.3 is then a down-bow.
% c425 (Gould, Behind Bars PDF 21: stay in one clef using up to at least three ledger lines; PDF 22: in continuous playing change clef only between phrases, after rests): the cellos' run at 209-210 (c407, A flat2-G flat4) stays in bass clef;
%   its top G flat4 needs three ledger lines. The tenor clef came at the 209|210 barline inside the slurred eighths and the bass clef before the rest on 210.4. Notes, slurs and MIDI unchanged.
% c521 (melody; Adler part 2 ch. 11, PDF 28-29, Ex. XI-13, 1812: the tune in trombones, horns, bassoons and lower strings against the upper strings' counterpoint): 205-206 the cellos leave the A flat pedal (kept in B.tbn, tuba, basses) and play F1's head at A flat 3 with Tbn 1-2 and Bn 2, an octave under the four horns, then return to A flat 2 at 207.
codaOneVc = { c'2\ff es'2 | d'2 f'2 | es'2 c'2 | e'2 bes2 | aes2 f2 | g4->\downbow g,4->\downbow r2 | aes2->(\f\downbow es4. f8 | ges4-.) f8( es8 des4-.) c4-. | aes,1 | aes,1 | aes,8(\< bes, c des) es( f ges aes | bes8 c' des' es') ges'4-> r4\! | }
codaOneCb = { \stemUp c,8\ff c,8 c,8 c,8 c,8 c,8 c,8 c,8 | d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 | es,8 es,8 es,8 es,8 es,8 es,8 es,8 es,8 | e,8 e,8 e,8 e,8 e,8 e,8 e,8 e,8 | f,8 f,8 f,8 f,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 d,8 | g,,4->\downbow g,,4->\downbow r2 | aes,,8\f aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 | aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 | aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 | aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 | \bnLow aes,,8\< aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 | aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8 aes,,8\! \stemNeutral | }
